1
00:00:01,520 --> 00:00:06,950
Let's move now to allocation methods. So basically,
let's see how we can answer the question:

2
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:12,000
how do we know where each file is?

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,220
First way to go with this is to choose contiguous
allocation.

4
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:22,970
It is as the name suggests, and it's similar
to what we have discussed when we were talking

5
00:00:22,970 --> 00:00:29,890
about memory. So you put in this case one
file after

6
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:39,190
each other an if you remove a file like here
then you leave a hole that can be potentially

7
00:00:39,190 --> 00:00:43,600
re-used later on by other files if they fit of
course.

8
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:50,390
It's easy to implement. Each file needs to
be associated basically with two numbers,

9
00:00:50,390 --> 00:00:55,430
the 1st and the last address or the first
address and the number of blocks.

10
00:00:55,430 --> 00:01:02,489
It's also good for performance if you are
doing a single scan reading and you scan from

11
00:01:02,489 --> 00:01:03,490
the beginning to the end.

12
00:01:03,490 --> 00:01:08,290
And this, of course, is true for rotating
disks, in which

13
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:12,710
close-by locations are faster to access than

14
00:01:12,710 --> 00:01:18,109
locations that are distant (if it is an SSD disk
it doesn't really matter that much)

15
00:01:18,109 --> 00:01:24,600
But you have fragmentation, OK?

16
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:31,270
Exactly as you saw with contiguous allocation
when we were discussing memory and of course

17
00:01:31,270 --> 00:01:36,149
you also need to know in advance the size
of the file

18
00:01:36,149 --> 00:01:41,079
which is not really realistic especially
because

19
00:01:41,079 --> 00:01:42,310
many fires can have a

20
00:01:42,310 --> 00:01:51,450
size that changes over time. So even if you
know the size when you want to store the

21
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:57,419
file, what will you do if then the file changes
sizes and it becomes larger than the number

22
00:01:57,419 --> 00:01:58,849
of blocks you actually have?

23
00:01:58,849 --> 00:02:01,549
Still, as always, there is no

24
00:02:01,549 --> 00:02:09,020
real good or real bad here. It really depends
on the setup. Contiguous allocation is used

25
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:15,810
when you keep stuff on compact discs and DVD's
and so on. Why? Because there you only write

26
00:02:15,810 --> 00:02:21,360
once, you don't really modify the stuff and
you know the size of all the files you have

27
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:26,270
to put and those are not going to change,
so as you can see this can be used in practice.

28
00:02:26,270 --> 00:02:32,500
It depends a bit on the case, the support.
What is the scenario you are taking into account.

29
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:38,150
An alternative way to go if you want to avoid
this problem of fragmentation is to use linked list

30
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:44,890
allocation. So you use a portion of each block
to point to the next block and use a special

31
00:02:44,890 --> 00:02:49,400
number to refer to the last block.

32
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:52,720
And then basically you can

33
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:59,190
scatter around the blocks of a file into
blocks in the disk that do not need to be

34
00:02:59,190 --> 00:03:05,360
contiguous. So you have the first block
O which points to the *second* block address

35
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:13,310
which is 7 in the physical device which
points to the physical block number 2 and

36
00:03:13,310 --> 00:03:16,670
so on so forth.

37
00:03:16,670 --> 00:03:21,120
Now you need to maintain only one
number per file, which is the address of the

38
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:22,120
first block.

39
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:28,931
But, on the other hand, you don't know
exactly where the block number N is, because

40
00:03:28,931 --> 00:03:32,540
now it's not contiguous. You cannot just sum n 
to the first one you actually need to

41
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:35,819
traverse the

42
00:03:35,819 --> 00:03:41,610
all the blocks until you find block N.

43
00:03:41,610 --> 00:03:46,460
As you can imagine, this is not really practical,
especially because if you have to traverse

44
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:50,831
all the blocks every time to find where a
specific block is and all the blocks are

45
00:03:50,831 --> 00:03:54,650
on disk you have to do a lot of context
switches to access the blocks on disk and

46
00:03:54,650 --> 00:03:58,380
so on. Probably this is not really

47
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:07,709
the best in the type of setups
also you waste now a small amount of bytes

48
00:04:07,709 --> 00:04:10,070
inside the

49
00:04:10,070 --> 00:04:13,319
the block because now you need to store the
pointer, so you cannot really use the entire

50
00:04:13,319 --> 00:04:19,930
block for your data for putting content of
the file, you need to keep part of it to maintain

51
00:04:19,930 --> 00:04:23,240
the pointer. It's not a big amount of

52
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:29,080
it's not a big portion of the block, but still
focus on the fact that we always have this

53
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:35,460
space and time tradeoffs, so in this case
you pay space

54
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:40,819
because you put it inside each block, you
need to take a portion of that block to keep

55
00:04:40,819 --> 00:04:44,289
the point out of the following one.

56
00:04:44,289 --> 00:04:47,990
You can optimize this, of course, and

57
00:04:47,990 --> 00:04:50,360
you can decide to load

58
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:56,360
the information about where each block is
and what is the next block in main memory

59
00:04:56,360 --> 00:05:01,030
instead of disk, and this is the file allocation
table

60
00:05:01,030 --> 00:05:04,219
So you can keep a list of all the physical
blocks.

61
00:05:04,219 --> 00:05:09,499
You can keep track of the address at which
each file starts as you were doing with a normal

62
00:05:09,499 --> 00:05:16,021
linked list, and then you can just traverse
this table in memory to find where each block

63
00:05:16,021 --> 00:05:23,180
is. It's still a sequential search, you need to
go 1 by 1 until you find the block you want,

64
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:26,990
but since you don't have to interact with
the disk and you keep all this information

65
00:05:26,990 --> 00:05:30,189
in memory then of course it becomes

66
00:05:30,189 --> 00:05:33,580
faster. Also, in this case you need to pay

67
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:43,629
a price, and again it's complexity in space,
because now you need to use part of your main

68
00:05:43,629 --> 00:05:48,659
memory too to keep track of this and you need
to load it when you boot up your operating

69
00:05:48,659 --> 00:05:54,150
system so it takes a bit of a longer time
to put up and so on. And here is just a simple

70
00:05:54,150 --> 00:06:00,430
example of how you can compute the size of
this table based on the disk size and the

71
00:06:00,430 --> 00:06:05,580
block size and how many bytes each address in
the

72
00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:10,860
file allocation table is taking.

73
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:17,849
OK, here you have the pluses and the minuses 

74
00:06:17,849 --> 00:06:20,889
so the pros and cons that we just discussed.

75
00:06:20,889 --> 00:06:27,300
Final one, which is used in Unix is to keep
I-nodes. So basically to associate a small

76
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:33,969
data structure to each file this data structure

77
00:06:33,969 --> 00:06:39,789
contains the attributes of each file.

78
00:06:39,789 --> 00:06:46,939
You load it in memory only when you are trying
to access the file and this data structure

79
00:06:46,939 --> 00:06:52,990
the I-node also contains an initial

80
00:06:52,990 --> 00:06:56,059
set of addresses. Let's say the
first eight blocks

81
00:06:56,059 --> 00:07:02,560
of the file, so that if you have to pick a
specific block out of the first eight blocks,

82
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:08,869
you have the addresses in memory and they're
pointing to the specific block on the disk.

83
00:07:08,869 --> 00:07:13,080
Of course, there is no guarantee that you
need eight blocks and at the same time there

84
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:18,800
is no guarantee that eight blocks are enough.
If you don't need them well you are wasting

85
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:23,379
some of them, but that's not a big deal, but
if you need more, of course you need a mechanism

86
00:07:23,379 --> 00:07:26,819
to find out also where the other addresses
are.

87
00:07:26,819 --> 00:07:31,159
And you can do this keeping an extra address

88
00:07:31,159 --> 00:07:37,879
to a block on the disk, but this is a special
block that is only used to store the address

89
00:07:37,879 --> 00:07:41,650
of the following blocks.

90
00:07:41,650 --> 00:07:46,770
And you can do this with different levels
of indirection, so this is level one. You

91
00:07:46,770 --> 00:07:53,939
have a pointer to a block that contains addresses.
You can have a pointer to a block that contains

92
00:07:53,939 --> 00:07:59,360
pointers to other blocks that are full of
addresses, and you can go up to three levels

93
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:02,509
of indirection.

94
00:08:02,509 --> 00:08:10,099
I will show you an example of this in the
exercise session.

95
00:08:10,099 --> 00:08:20,330
Now, if you only load I-nodes for the files
you actually open, they take less memory

96
00:08:20,330 --> 00:08:27,300
you need to maintain a smaller amount of information
in memory about the files that are open, and

97
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:33,349
it's also proportional actually to the number
of files that you have opened,

98
00:08:33,349 --> 00:08:37,450
but it becomes complicated again when you
have very large files because of these mechanisms

99
00:08:37,450 --> 00:08:44,010
of pointing to a block that contains pointers
and so on. So it works well as long

100
00:08:44,010 --> 00:08:48,900
as the majority of files have / 

101
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:49,900
take a number of

102
00:08:49,900 --> 00:08:54,450
blocks that is more or less covered by the
ones that are already made available directly

103
00:08:54,450 --> 00:08:55,880
in the I-node.

